10:32:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is a very common claim—but “the brain finishes developing at 25” is more of a popular shorthand than an established biological fact. The claim largely came from MRI research showing that, on average, regions including the prefrontal cortex continue changing structurally and functionally through adolescence and into the twenties. Researchers observed ongoing myelination and changes in connectivity, and the media often translated that into: “the brain isn’t fully developed until 25.” But the actual findings are more nuanced: - There is **no universal age-25 cutoff**. - Different brain systems mature on different timelines. - The studies show broad averages, with large individual variation. - Brain structure and function continue changing after 25, just generally less dramatically than during childhood and adolescence. - “Development” does not mean someone under 25 is incapable of responsible judgment; it means some average cognitive and emotional-control systems are still maturing. Car-rental policies are not really based on a neuroscientific declaration that brains mature at 25. The common minimum age and under-25 surcharge are mainly tied to **insurance and accident-risk statistics**: younger drivers, especially teens and early-twenties drivers, historically have higher crash rates and more costly claims on average. Twenty-five became a conventional pricing and eligibility threshold, not a precise brain-development milestone. So: the underlying observation—brain maturation continues into the twenties—is real. The tidy statement that it “stops at 25” is not.
