10:33:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It doesn’t mean Psych 101 is untrustworthy. It means many introductory facts are **compressed summaries of probabilistic research**, not literal rules with sharp boundaries. That is true across much of science: - “Humans have 206 bones” is useful, though some people have a different number. - “Adults sleep 7–9 hours” is evidence-based, though needs vary. - “Blood pressure rises with age” is true on average, not for every person. - “The brain develops into the mid-20s” is a valid broad summary, even though 25 is not a universal endpoint. Psychology especially deals with traits and behavior, where individual variation and context are enormous. Good teaching should distinguish: 1. **The robust core:** executive-control-related brain systems and behavior typically continue maturing beyond adolescence, often into the twenties. 2. **The useful shorthand:** “the brain isn’t fully developed until around 25.” 3. **The overstatement:** everyone’s brain suddenly finishes developing at exactly age 25, and nothing meaningfully changes afterward. A Psych 101 instructor may simplify because the course has to cover broad patterns before methods, caveats, and competing models. That simplification becomes a problem only if it is presented as an absolute fact or used to make overly strong claims about an individual’s competence, responsibility, or future. So the lesson is not “trust nothing.” It is: trust the underlying finding, but treat tidy one-number claims as teaching shorthand unless you see the definition, measurement, and evidence behind them.
